# Sprint 4 — Web-Based Financial Model / Raspberry Pi Deployment

**Updated:** September 29, 2026

This is the current Sprint 4 handoff document. It records what was planned, what has been completed, what artifacts were produced, and exactly how to resume when the Raspberry Pi is available.

---

# 1. Sprint Objective

Sprint 4 adds a web-based implementation of the Financial Model that can ultimately run on a Raspberry Pi 5.

The existing Windows Tkinter desktop application remains operational.

Target architecture:

```text
Windows Desktop (Tkinter)          Web Browser
        |                              |
        | existing/local               | HTTP/HTTPS
        v                              v
     Services <-------------------- FastAPI
        |                              |
        +---------- SQLAlchemy --------+
                       |
                     SQLite
                       |
                Raspberry Pi 5
```

Long-term, the Raspberry Pi is the single owner of SQLite. Clients communicate through the API rather than opening SQLite over a network share.

---

# 2. Critical Development Principles

Sprint 4 is additive.

Do not:

- replace the Tkinter GUI,
- remove existing screens,
- rewrite working services unnecessarily,
- expose SQLite over a network share,
- change the schema without a demonstrated requirement,
- remove existing functionality,
- rewrite BMO/Nesbitt importers merely for the web application,
- expose an unauthenticated FastAPI port directly to the public Internet.

Use existing services from the web/API layer. Keep financial/business calculations out of route functions.

Use `Decimal` for financial values.

