# 15. Stage 4.9B — Actual Pi Deployment — NEXT

Do this only when the Pi/NVMe environment is available.

## Step 1 — Prepare Pi/NVMe

Install the official M.2 HAT+ and 2242 NVMe SSD.

Install/boot the supported Raspberry Pi OS configuration from NVMe.

## Step 2 — Copy the tested project

Copy the complete tested Financial Model project to the Pi.

Also copy the existing database:

```text
database/financial_model.db
```

Do not replace it with an empty database.

## Step 3 — Back up database

Before deployment/migration work, create a backup of the existing database.

## Step 4 — Check database/migration state

Do not blindly migrate.

Inspect:

```bash
alembic current
alembic heads
```

If schema changes are not required, leave the database untouched.

## Step 5 — Install the application

From the project root:

```bash
sudo ./deploy/install_pi.sh <application-directory> <service-user>
```

Example from the prepared documentation:

```bash
sudo ./deploy/install_pi.sh /home/ron/financialModel ron
```

Use the real username/path on the Pi.

## Step 6 — Verify systemd

```bash
systemctl status financial-model
journalctl -u financial-model -n 100 --no-pager
```

## Step 7 — Verify health locally

```bash
curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health
```

Expected:

```json
{"status":"ok"}
```

## Step 8 — Verify LAN web access

Get the Pi IP:

```bash
hostname -I
```

From a PC/phone on the same LAN:

```text
http://<pi-ip>:8000/
```

Test every screen:

```text
Portfolio
Portfolio History
Account Management
Account History
Holdings History
Import
```

## Step 9 — Test imports

Use known-good BMO and Nesbitt Excel files.

Confirm:

- import works,
- duplicate behavior is preserved,
- account/snapshot data is correct,
- holdings and cash appear correctly.

## Step 10 — Test market data/valuation

Confirm the Pi can reach Yahoo Finance/yfinance and the Portfolio page displays current saved valuation data correctly.

## Step 11 — Test database ownership

Confirm the Pi is using its local NVMe database.

Do not mount the database from Windows/NAS/network share.

## Step 12 — Configure WireGuard

Use the 4.9A templates as a starting point.

Generate real keys.

Replace placeholders with the actual:

- Pi LAN address,
- home LAN subnet,
- WireGuard subnet,
- Pi WireGuard address,
- client WireGuard address,
- endpoint/public IP or DNS name,
- router port-forwarding information if required.

Do not expose FastAPI port 8000 directly to the Internet.

## Step 13 — Remote test

From a device outside the home LAN:

1. Enable WireGuard.
2. Verify the tunnel.
3. Open the Financial Model through the Pi's WireGuard address.
4. Test all web screens.
5. Test an import if practical.
6. Confirm the application remains inaccessible through an unintended public TCP 8000 path.

## Step 14 — Record actual results

Record:

- Pi OS version,
- Python version,
- NVMe model/capacity,
- Pi IP,
- WireGuard subnet,
- FastAPI service status,
- test count/result,
- any deployment issues.

Do not put private WireGuard keys into these documentation files.

